## Concrete correctness choices

HH:MM means exactly zero seconds and milliseconds. Formatting candidate local times only through the minute hid historical four-to-56-second gaps and created false folds. The site now uses the exact delivered SDK approach: include second and fractional-second fields in native Intl wall-time comparison, compare to HH:MM:00.000, and round offset ×60,000 at millisecond precision before UTC subtraction to eliminate floating-point conversion noise. It does not round away historical seconds, alter sampled offsets, invent flags, change fold/gap policy, widen input validation or add a second receipt validator.

The 16 original historical counterexamples retain their actual gap shifts and become receipt-compatible with honest dst-gap flags. Adjacent ordinary times no longer acquire false fold flags. Existing known/unknown time, fractional LMT, non-hour gaps, skipped-day and Gregorian year-zero controls remain explicit. For identical explicit UTC inputs, the numerical kernel is unchanged; correcting a previously misresolved historical input may intentionally change its instant/flags and resulting chart.

The receipt companion preserves the refusal boundary for callers deliberately supplying old inconsistent flags. Positive cases check one public natal call, matching chart projection and SDK-validated receipt/replay context. No receipt runtime, user action, storage, account API, design, navigation, Registry or Astrofolio logic was modified.

localDateEndpointsUtc and all its policy/caller code remain unchanged. Its separate empty/disconnected civil-date interval defects are not solved or certified by this precision adoption. Signed fixed-offset receipt syntax and exact-pole receipt coverage also remain declared limitations. No broad astronomical or historical accuracy claim follows from these finite tests.

